# {{name}}

Your custom [OpenChoreo](https://openchoreo.dev) Portal — a thin
[Backstage](https://backstage.io) app built on the published
`@openchoreo/backstage-portal-app` and `@openchoreo/backstage-portal-backend`
packages, pinned to portal release `{{version}}` (the `resolutions` in the
root `package.json` hold every `@openchoreo/*` package to it).

You own this repository: add plugins, replace pages, and re-brand without
forking the OpenChoreo monorepo.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 20 or 22, and Yarn (via `corepack enable`; the repo pins its own
  Yarn under `.yarn/releases`).
- `@openchoreo/*` packages resolve from `{{{registry}}}` (see `.yarnrc.yml`).
  If you point that at a private registry, set an `npmAuthToken` for the
  `openchoreo` scope in your user-level `~/.yarnrc.yml` — don't commit
  registry tokens to this repo. Only the `openchoreo` scope uses it; to
  install every other package through a mirror too, set
  `npmRegistryServer` as well.

## Local development

```sh
yarn install
yarn start   # frontend on :3000, backend on :7007
```

Local settings live in `app-config.local.yaml` (gitignored; the scaffold
seeded it from `app-config.local.yaml.example`). Point `openchoreo.baseUrl`,
`thunder.baseUrl`, and the `auth.providers.openchoreo-auth` URLs at your
OpenChoreo control plane — the defaults target a local k3d install on
`*.openchoreo.localhost:8080`.

## Adding plugins

Optional OpenChoreo plugins, community Backstage plugins, and your in-house
plugins are all the same thing: packages this portal depends on.

- **Frontend**: `yarn workspace app add <package>`. That's the whole
  installation: `app.packages: all` turns on [feature
  discovery](https://backstage.io/docs/frontend-system/building-apps/installing-plugins),
  which installs any dependency of `packages/app` that default-exports a
  frontend feature from its root or `./alpha` entry point. A plugin that only
  has named exports goes into `createApp({ features: [...] })` in
  `packages/app/src/App.tsx` instead.
- **Backend**: `yarn workspace backend add <package>`, then
  `backend.add(import('<package>'))` in `packages/backend/src/index.ts`.
- **In-house**: scaffold into `plugins/` with `yarn new`.

## Customizing the portal

Everything the portal shell and the OpenChoreo plugins contribute is a
Backstage [New Frontend System](https://backstage.io/docs/frontend-system/)
extension, so most changes are configuration, not code:

```yaml
app:
  extensions:
    # Hide a tab or a page you don't use
    - entity-content:openchoreo-observability/wirelogs: false
    # Move a tab into a different group
    - entity-content:openchoreo-ci/workflows:
        config:
          group: development
```

To replace a piece outright, override its extension with a frontend module
for the plugin that owns it. A module swaps only the extensions it lists and
keeps the rest of the plugin. Don't create a second plugin with the same
plugin ID instead: Backstage keeps only one of the two, so the original
plugin's other extensions and APIs disappear. An extension ID is
`<kind>:<plugin ID>/<name>`; give your extension the same kind and name, and
the module the same plugin ID. The app already
depends on the two Backstage packages this needs, at the versions the portal
uses:

```tsx
// packages/app/src/App.tsx
import { createFrontendModule } from '@backstage/frontend-plugin-api';
import { EntityContentBlueprint } from '@backstage/plugin-catalog-react/alpha';

// Replaces entity-content:openchoreo-ci/workflows
const buildTab = EntityContentBlueprint.make({
  name: 'workflows',
  params: {
    path: '/workflows',
    title: 'Build',
    loader: () => import('./BuildTab').then(m => <m.BuildTab />),
  },
});

export default createApp({
  features: [
    createFrontendModule({ pluginId: 'openchoreo-ci', extensions: [buildTab] }),
  ],
  // ...
}).createRoot();
```

The new extension replaces the original completely, so set any of its
parameters you still want (such as `filter` or `group`).

Optional OpenChoreo capabilities are switched with
`openchoreo.features.*.enabled` in `app-config.yaml`; keep them in sync with
what your OpenChoreo control plane actually runs.

## Branding

Logo, product name, and brand color are runtime configuration — no rebuild:

```yaml
app:
  branding:
    name: Acme Portal
    iconLogo: data:image/svg+xml;base64,…
    fullLogo: data:image/svg+xml;base64,…
    theme:
      light:
        primaryColor: '#0f766e'
      dark:
        primaryColor: '#2dd4bf'
```

Browser-chrome assets (favicons, PWA manifest) live in `packages/app/public/`
and are yours to replace.

## Building the image

The scaffold ships a `yarn.lock` seeded with the dependency set portal
release `{{version}}` was tested with; the first `yarn install` trims it to
what this portal uses. The CLI commits that change; if you created the
portal with `--skip-install`, commit the updated lockfile yourself. Then:

```sh
docker build . -f packages/backend/Dockerfile \
  -t registry.example.com/acme/portal:0.1.0
```

Deploy it by pointing the OpenChoreo Helm chart at your image:

```sh
helm upgrade openchoreo-control-plane … \
  --set backstage.image.repository=registry.example.com/acme/portal \
  --set backstage.image.tag=0.1.0
```

## Upgrading to a new portal release

Every release is published as a tag of the
[`openchoreo/portal-template`](https://github.com/openchoreo/portal-template)
repo. Merging that tag brings in everything the release changed — the
`@openchoreo/*` versions, configs, the Dockerfile, scaffolder templates and
`.openchoreo-portal.json` — while git keeps your own changes.

Once, right after scaffolding, record the template release you started
from (`.openchoreo-portal.json` names it) as the merge base. This merge
changes no files — it only links the histories:

```sh
git remote add portal-template https://github.com/openchoreo/portal-template
git fetch portal-template --tags
git merge -s ours --allow-unrelated-histories v{{version}} \
  -m "chore: record portal-template v{{version}} as the upgrade base"
```

For every upgrade after that:

```sh
git fetch portal-template --tags
git merge <new release tag>   # e.g. v{{version}} -> the next release
```

Resolve any conflicts (they only appear where you changed the same lines as
the release). For `yarn.lock`, take the release's version
(`git checkout --theirs yarn.lock`) and let `yarn install` re-add your own
dependencies. Then run `yarn install && yarn tsc && yarn test:all` and
rebuild your image.
